</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">RephQA</span> to conduct a comprehensive investigation into the <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RE</span>adability of LLMs in <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>ublic <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H</span>ealth <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q</span>uestion-<span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>nswering (QA) tasks.
A key strength of <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RephQA</span> lies in its expert-grounded foundation: it comprises of high-quality test set with 533 QA pairs curated from 27 online public health resources, all reviewed by professionals in nursing, public health, and clinical communication.
Different from prior related benchmarks (Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ RephQA: Evaluating Readability of Large Language Models in Public Health Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RephQA</span> focuses on frequently asked public health questions from lay users or patients, and introduces a novel proxy multiple-choice QA that effectively evaluates the accuracy of generated responses.
Besides, we collect a large-scale corpus of 36,060 public health questions to serve as a post-training dataset, supporting the exploration of readability-enhancement strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate 25 LLMs on <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RephQA</span> using two readability metrics (Flesch-Kincaid grade level <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kincaid et al., 1975</a>)</cite> and professional score) and one accuracy metric based on our proxy multiple-choice QA.
Additionally, we benchmark four strategies for improving the readability of LLM outputs, including standard prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malik et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, chain-of-thought prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and our proposed token-adapted GRPO.
Experimental results show that most LLMs fail to produce responses at a desirable readability level.
Furthermore, post-training with readability-oriented rewards significantly improves readability while maintaining accuracy, where the token-adapted GRPO achieves the best performance.
In summary, our contributions are listed as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RephQA</span> for evaluating the readability of LLM-generated responses in public health QA tasks aimed at lay users and patients, with two readability metrics and one accuracy metric evaluated by our proposed proxy multi-choice QA.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of 25 LLMs on <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RephQA</span>, revealing that most models struggle to generate responses with an expected readability, despite achieving high accuracy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explore three existing strategies to improve LLM readability and propose a novel method, the token-adapted GRPO method, which achieves the best performance and provides new insights into readability-aware generation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RephQA</span> Benchmark</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Dataset Curation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert-Grounded Public Health QA </span>
We collected a diverse set of publicly available healthcare-related QA pairs from 27 authoritative online resources covering a broad range of public health topics, including general health, cardiovascular disease, diabetes, HIV, diet, exercise, alcohol, smoking, sleep, mental health, sexual health, and health equity. Sources were selected for their accessibility to lay audiences and their coverage of frequently asked questions by the general public. The constructed datasets are reviewed and refined by domain experts. Based on the question intent, we classify them into four categories: <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suggestion</span>, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fact</span>, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Definition</span>, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rationale</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proxy Multiple Choice QA </span>
Prior studies have attempted to use LLMs as judges when rating the accuracy or relevance of a response relative to a reference answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, these approaches are not reliable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schroeder and Wood-Doughty, 2025</a>)</cite>. To bridge this gap, we further construct a multiple-choice QA based on each reference answer. Specifically, we design an agent workflow that proposes and iteratively refines the proposed QA pairs. This QA task could be an effective proxy for the accuracy evaluation of the original QA: if a model’s response closely matches the original reference answer, an LLM agent will also produce a correct answer for the proxy QA.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics </span>
We use the Flesch-Kincaid grade level <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kincaid et al., 1975</a>)</cite> as one of the readability scores. We also define professional score as the percentage of professional terms in the text. Accuracy is measured through the aforementioned proxy multiple-choice QA task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-Training Dataset </span>
For the post-training dataset, we curated a large-scale collection consisting of 36,060 public health questions.
These queries span an extensive range of public health topics, including sleep, exercise, smoking, weight, diet, HIV care, mental health, cardiovascular, diabetes, cholesterol, and SOGI, from 55 publicly available resources, which were manually selected by nursing and public health professionals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Readability-Enhancement Strategies</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token-adapted GRPO </span>
Besides benchmarking existing post-training or test-time-scaling methods, we further propose <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Token-adapted GRPO</span> (TA-GRPO). Different from the standard GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which operates at the sentence level, assigning a uniform reward for all the tokens, we assign higher weights for professional medical terms.
Specifically, at each optimization step, the LLM generates a group of responses <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{o}_{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{o}_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{o}_{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{o}_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{o}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{o}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th generated response. For <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th token in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th response, a token-level reward is computed based on whether it appears in our curated professional vocabulary: <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,t}^{\prime}=w_{i,t}Tr_{i}/\sum_{t^{\prime}}^{T}w_{i,t^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,t}^{\prime}=w_{i,t}Tr_{i}/\sum_{t^{\prime}}^{T}w_{i,t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,t}=1+\delta(\boldsymbol{o}_{i,t})w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,t}=1+\delta(\boldsymbol{o}_{i,t})w</annotation></semantics></math>, controling the penalty strength for professional terms, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the original sentence-level reward for the response <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{o}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{o}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum length of the responses, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\boldsymbol{o}_{i,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\boldsymbol{o}_{i,t})</annotation></semantics></math> is an indicator function that returns 1 if the token <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{o}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{o}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to a professional term, and 0 otherwise. The advantage function is redefined as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i,t}=\frac{r_{i,t}^{\prime}-\operatorname{mean}(\{r_{1},\cdots,r_{G}\})}{\operatorname{std}(\{r_{1},\cdots,r_{G}\})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i,t}=\frac{r_{i,t}^{\prime}-\operatorname{mean}(\{r_{1},\cdots,r_{G}\})}{\operatorname{std}(\{r_{1},\cdots,r_{G}\})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use the three metrics in <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Dataset Curation ‣ 2. RephQA Benchmark ‣ RephQA: Evaluating Readability of Large Language Models in Public Health Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> as the reward signal. We use the token-adapted objective for the professional score, while retaining the original GRPO for other metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Experiment</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup </span>
For proprietary models, we consider GPT-4 series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, GPT-4o series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, o series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, Claude <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2024</a>)</cite>, Gemini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite>
For open source models, we consider LLaMA 3.1 series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Qwen 2.5 series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite>, Phi-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Mistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, BioMistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labrak et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, InternLM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Gemma-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, DeepSeek-V2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Yi-1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
During evaluation, we set target thresholds for each metric: a Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level of 6, a professional score of 0, and 100% accuracy. A grade level of 6 corresponds to a 5th-grade reading level, while a professional score of 0 indicates no use of medical jargon.</p>
</div>
