<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarking Results </span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3. Experiment ‣ RephQA: Evaluating Readability of Large Language Models in Public Health Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, existing models generally struggle to generate responses at a targeted readability level.
Among all models, <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-32B</span> achieves the lowest Flesch-Kincaid grade level, while <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude-3.7-Sonnet</span> outputs the highest, and <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA3.1-8B</span> comes closest to the target value of 6. For professional score, <span id="S3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BioMistral-7B</span> uses the most specialized language—likely due to medical fine-tuning—whereas <span id="S3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma3-12b</span> exhibits the least, enhancing readability. In terms of accuracy, <span id="S3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o4-mini</span> performs best at 83.68%. Among QA types, <span id="S3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">definition</span> questions are easiest (84.06% accuracy), while <span id="S3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fact</span> questions are most challenging (72.80%). The <span id="S3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">suggestion</span> category shows the lowest grade level and jargon use, consistent with its simpler language requirements.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Benchmarking different readability-enhancement strategies with different backbone LLMs. <span id="S3.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">+Prompt</span> means simply instructing the LLM to generate responses with desired properties. <span id="S3.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">+CoT</span> refers to Chain-of-Thoughts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
</figcaption>
