<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Readability-Enhancement Strategies </span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3. Experiment ‣ RephQA: Evaluating Readability of Large Language Models in Public Health Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, CoT prompting produces less readable responses than others, as indicated by a <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> increase in grade score and a <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>57.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> increase in professional score.
The accuracy difference between GRPO and TA-GRPO is minimal (<math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>), likely due to the nature of our benchmark, which emphasizes readability over complex reasoning. Both methods significantly improve readability compared to the base model. Moreover, TA-GRPO outperforms vanilla GRPO in professional score optimization, achieving a <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="53.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>53.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">53.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> average improvement while maintaining a similar grade level (within <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.0\%</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
